     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Does a heat pump need refrigerant added every summer?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A heat pump should not need a routine summer refrigerant refill. Low refrigerant calls for an assessment of the cause, including a possible leak. Adding refrigerant without addressing the cause can leave the cooling problem unresolved. The refrigerant type and repair scope affect the next steps."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Can a reversing valve problem look like an AC failure?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes. A heat pump uses a reversing valve to change the direction of refrigerant flow between heating and cooling. A control problem or valve fault can prevent that change. Warm airflow alone does not identify a failed valve; the settings, airflow, and operating conditions also need attention."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Can central AC be replaced with a heat pump?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A ducted heat pump can be considered when replacing central AC, but the indoor equipment, controls, and ducts must suit the proposed system. City & Suburban installs American Standard equipment. A replacement discussion should distinguish the cooling project from the separate plan for winter heating."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What should a summer heat pump service request include?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Useful details include the model, current thermostat mode, rooms affected, visible ice or water, and when cooling last worked normally. City & Suburban offers same-day service when the schedule allows. Roof access or locked equipment areas should be mentioned when arranging a visit."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is heat pump maintenance the same as AC maintenance?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "The cooling side shares airflow, heat-transfer, and drainage concerns with an air conditioner. A heat pump also changes between cooling and heating modes. Seasonal service should account for the complete system. City & Suburban offers maintenance plans with seasonal check-ups, priority dispatch, and member discounts."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Should a frozen heat pump keep running to cool the home?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Continued cooling operation can add ice and reduce airflow further. Stop cooling and arrange service rather than chipping ice or handling refrigerant connections. A homeowner can note the filter condition and blocked vents. Ice alone does not prove that refrigerant is low or identify the repair needed."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What determines the cost of heat pump cooling repair?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "The fault, component availability, refrigerant type, equipment access, and condition of the indoor and outdoor equipment affect the repair scope. City & Suburban offers upfront pricing with no hidden fees. A symptom such as weak cooling does not establish a price before the cause is known."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Does poor cooling mean the heat pump needs replacement?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Poor cooling alone does not justify replacement. A correctable airflow or control issue differs from recurring refrigerant leaks or several major faults. City & Suburban repairs all makes and models. Compare the identified repair with the condition and compatibility of the complete system before deciding."
     }
    },
